@chapter 5 | Light and Optics

@intro
Light is the single largest physics topic in UPPSC. The questions cover the **visible range** and the eye's peak sensitivity, the **speed of light**, **refraction** (apparent depth, flattened Sun), **total internal reflection** (optical fibre, mirage, diamond), **scattering** (blue sky, red danger signals, black sky in space), **dispersion and the rainbow**, **mirrors and lenses** in daily use, and the **human eye** and its defects. It contains **{pyqs} PYQs and {ones} one-liners**.
@end

@section 5.1 | Nature and Speed of Light

@src 5:1
@pyq U.P.P.C.S.(Mains) 2005
The wave length extension of visible light is
(a) Between 200-900 nm
(b) Between 250-850 nm
(c) Between 300-800 nm
(d) Between 390-780 nm
@ans (d)
@end

@q 5:2-4

The eye sees roughly **390 (violet) to 780 nm (red)**, that is 3,900-7,800 Å. In daylight it is most sensitive at about **555 nm (5,500 Å), the yellow-green**; that is why fire engines, high-visibility jackets and tennis balls are often lime-yellow. The ear is most sensitive at about **1,000-4,000 Hz**. **Persistence of vision** is about **1/16 second**, which lets 24 frames per second look like continuous motion in cinema; persistence of hearing is about 1/10 second.

@q 5:5-9

@table
Medium | Speed of light (approx.)
**Vacuum** | **3 × 10^{8} m/s** (299,792 km/s), the maximum possible
Air | Almost the same as vacuum
Water | 2.25 × 10^{8} m/s
Glass | 2 × 10^{8} m/s
**Diamond** | 1.24 × 10^{8} m/s (refractive index 2.42)
@end

Nothing travels faster than light in vacuum, not cosmic-ray particles, electrons or rockets. Sunlight takes about **8 minutes 20 seconds** to reach the Earth and moonlight about 1.3 seconds. Light travels in straight lines because its **wavelength is very small**, so it hardly bends (diffracts) around ordinary objects.

@q 5:10

The **Moon** is non-luminous: it only reflects sunlight. Stars, flames and lamps are luminous.

@section 5.2 | Refraction and Total Internal Reflection

@q 5:11-12

When light passes from one medium to another, its **speed and wavelength change but frequency stays the same**; the change in speed bends the ray (**refraction**). Refraction makes a pool look **shallower** (apparent depth = real depth / refractive index), a stick look bent in water, stars **twinkle**, and the Sun appear **flattened (elliptical)** near the horizon and visible about 2 minutes before actual sunrise and after sunset.

@q 5:20

@q 5:13-14

**Total internal reflection (TIR)** happens when light going from a denser to a rarer medium meets the boundary at more than the **critical angle**; all of it reflects back. **Optical fibres** use TIR to carry light signals through thin glass strands with very little loss, high bandwidth and no electrical (radio-frequency) interference. The key (a) treats statement 4 (about Reliance) as incorrect.

@q 5:15-16

@table
Effect | Cause
**Mirage** (water on a hot road, desert) | Refraction through hot, rarer air layers ending in **total internal reflection**
Sparkle of a **cut diamond** | **TIR**: very high refractive index (2.42), small critical angle (24°)
Optical fibre, endoscope | TIR
**Twinkling** of stars | Refraction by moving air layers
Pool looks shallow, pencil looks bent | Refraction
Flattened Sun at the horizon, early sunrise | Atmospheric refraction
**Blue sky**, red sunset | Scattering
**Rainbow** | Dispersion + refraction + total internal reflection in raindrops
Colours of soap bubbles and oil films | **Interference**
Bending of light around edges | Diffraction
Polaroid sunglasses | Polarisation
@end

@q 5:17

**Holography** records the full light wave (amplitude and phase) using **laser** light, giving a **3-D image**; holograms are used on credit cards, banknotes and passports as security features.

@section 5.3 | Colour, Scattering and Dispersion

@q 5:18-19

@qnote 5:18 | The source keys **ultraviolet**. Eye specialists describe eclipse-blindness (solar retinopathy) as mainly a **photochemical** injury from intense visible (blue) and ultraviolet light, together with heat from infrared; the retina has no pain receptors, so the damage is not felt.

A star's colour shows its **surface temperature**: **blue** stars are hottest (above 10,000 K), white and **yellow** (the Sun, about 5,800 K) are medium, and **red** stars are coolest (about 3,000 K).

@q 5:21-22

@q 5:23-25

**Dispersion**: white light splits into **VIBGYOR** in a prism because each colour has a different speed in glass. **Violet** has the shortest wavelength (about 400 nm), is slowed most and **deviated most**; **red** (about 700 nm) is **deviated least**. The colour of light is fixed by its **wavelength** (or frequency).

@q 5:26-27

**Rayleigh scattering** is inversely proportional to the **fourth power of wavelength** (1/λ^{4}). Blue light is scattered about 5.5 times more than red, so the **sky looks blue**. At sunrise and sunset sunlight crosses a long path of air, most blue is scattered away and the Sun looks **red-orange**. Red, scattered least, travels farthest through haze and fog, so it is used for **danger signals**, brake lights and aviation warning lamps.

@src1 5:4
@one
The sky appears blue because
@ans shorter wavelengths are scattered by the atmosphere more than longer wavelengths.
@end

@q 5:28

Above the atmosphere there is nothing to scatter sunlight, so astronauts see a **black sky** even in daytime; from a very high-flying aircraft the sky also looks dark.

@q 5:29-30

An opaque object's colour is the colour it **reflects**; it absorbs the rest. Grass reflects green and absorbs red and blue (which chlorophyll uses for photosynthesis). A **white** surface reflects all colours (cool clothes for summer) and a **black** one absorbs all (warm in winter).

@q 5:31-32

The **Doppler effect** shifts the light of a source moving away towards longer wavelength (**red shift**). Edwin Hubble (1929) found almost all galaxies are red-shifted, so the **universe is expanding**. A **black hole**'s gravity is so strong that even light cannot escape from inside its *event horizon*; the first direct image (of M87*) came from the Event Horizon Telescope in 2019.

@q 5:33

The rainbow runs **red on the outer edge to violet on the inner edge**, with **green in the middle**. It is always seen with the **Sun behind** the observer, at about 42° from the anti-solar point.

@section 5.4 | Mirrors and Lenses

@q 5:34-38

An **air bubble** in water is a convex-shaped region of a *rarer* medium, so it **diverges** light like a **concave lens**. Two parallel mirrors give **infinite** images; mirrors at angle θ give (360/θ - 1) images. To see your full height in a plane mirror you need one **half your height** (1.5 m person → 0.75 m mirror). A plane mirror is a sphere of **infinite** radius of curvature, so its power is zero.

@q 5:44-47

@table
Mirror / lens | Used in
**Concave mirror** | Car headlights, searchlights, torches (bulb at focus gives a parallel beam), shaving and make-up mirrors, **doctors' head mirror** (ENT), solar cookers, reflecting telescopes, dentists' mirrors
**Convex mirror** | **Rear-view mirror** of vehicles (wide field, erect diminished image), shop security mirrors, street-corner mirrors
Plane mirror | Looking glass, periscope, kaleidoscope
**Convex lens** | Magnifying glass, camera, microscope, correcting **hypermetropia** and presbyopia
**Concave lens** | Correcting **myopia**, peepholes in doors
**Cylindrical lens** | Correcting **astigmatism**
@end

@q 5:48-50

The **power** of a lens is 1/f (f in metres), measured in **dioptres**: convex lenses have **positive** and concave lenses **negative** power. Sunglasses have **zero** power; they only cut glare. A convex and a concave lens of equal focal length together give zero total power, acting like a **plane glass sheet**. An astronomical telescope gives an **inverted, virtual, magnified** final image; a terrestrial telescope adds an erecting lens.

@section 5.5 | The Human Eye

@q 5:51-52

The **eye works like a camera**: cornea and lens focus light, the **iris** is the diaphragm, the **pupil** is the aperture and the **retina** is the film (now the sensor). The image on the retina is **real, inverted and diminished**; the brain turns it upright.

@q 5:43

**Accommodation**: the **ciliary muscles** change the **curvature (convexity) of the eye lens**, and so its focal length, to focus near and far objects. The **least distance of distinct vision** for a normal eye is **25 cm**, and the far point is infinity.

@q 5:39-42

@table
Defect | What happens | Corrected by
**Myopia** (short-sightedness) | Far objects blurred; image forms **in front of** the retina | **Concave** lens
**Hypermetropia** (long-sightedness) | Near objects blurred; image forms **behind** the retina | **Convex** lens
**Presbyopia** | Near vision weakens with age as the lens stiffens | Convex or **bifocal** lens
**Astigmatism** | Uneven curvature of the cornea | **Cylindrical** lens
**Cataract** | Lens becomes cloudy | Surgery (artificial intra-ocular lens)
@end

@trap
Eye is most sensitive to **yellow-green (555 nm)**, not red. Frequency **does not change** on refraction. **Mirage, diamond sparkle and optical fibre = total internal reflection.** **Violet deviates most, red least.** Red is used for danger because it is **scattered least**. An **air bubble acts as a concave lens**. Rear-view mirrors are **convex**, headlights **concave**. **Myopia → concave lens, hypermetropia → convex lens.** Doctors' ENT mirror is **concave**.
@end

@heatmap

@pattern
Optics gives two to four questions in most science sections. **Total internal reflection, scattering and mirror uses** are asked almost every year, often in repeated wording (mirage, diamond, rear-view mirror, myopia). Recent papers (2021-2023) add the eye's sensitivity, refraction in a swimming pool and luminous bodies.
@end

